## Introduction

Grout is the smallest material on a tiling job and the one most likely to fail in view. It is also the most mis-sold: sanded versus unsanded, flexible versus rigid, and epoxy options nobody mentions until the wall has already stained. The gaps between your tiles live or die by these choices.

This guide explains grout selection by joint width and tile type, colour choices that age well in Nigerian dust conditions, correct mixing and application technique, the curing window before wet use, and sealing — the maintenance step that multiplies grout lifespan and that most installations quietly skip.

## What Grout Actually Does — and What It Doesn't

Grout fills the joint between tiles, and it does three jobs at once: it locks the tiles into a continuous surface so loads spread across the floor instead of point-loading one tile; it absorbs the small movements that every building makes as temperatures and moisture cycle; and it closes the gaps that would otherwise collect dirt and water at the tile edges.

What grout does not do is waterproof anything. Standard cement grout absorbs water — slowly, but genuinely — which is why a wet area's water management lives in the waterproofing membrane underneath, never in the joint line. Anyone selling grout as the waterproofing layer is selling the cheapest component as the most critical one. The full wet-area assembly is covered in [our bathroom waterproofing guide](/learn/how-to-tile-bathroom-waterproofing-best-practices); grout's role in it is to be clean, full, and flexible — nothing more.

This distinction matters because it drives every selection decision that follows. Grout that is asked only to be grout — full, correctly coloured, sealed, and flexible — performs for decades. Grout that is asked to be a water barrier fails and takes the installation's reputation with it.

## The Grout Types Available in the Nigerian Market

Walk into a building market and ask for grout and you will be handed a cement-based powder. That is the default, and it is the right default for most jobs — but only if you know which of the three families on the shelf you are being sold:

| Type | Made of | Best for | Watch out for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unsanded cement grout | Fine cement powder, no aggregate | Narrow joints under 3 mm, polished soft stone | Weak in wide joints; shrinks and cracks |
| Sanded cement grout | Cement plus fine sand | Joints 3 mm and wider, floors, most walls | Sand scratches soft polished stone; can be harsh to tool on walls |
| Polymer-modified grout | Cement with latex/polymer additive | Wet areas, floors with underfloor movement, exteriors | Slightly higher price; still needs sealing |
| Epoxy grout | Resin + hardener, no cement | Showers, kitchens, high-stain areas, commercial floors | Fast pot life, harder to apply, 2–4× the price |

The polymer-modified category is where most quality residential jobs land: the additive makes the grout denser and more flexible, which buys real resistance to the cracking that plain sanded grout shows along wall-floor junctions and in rooms with any movement. The difference in price between plain and modified is small relative to the cost of re-grouting.

Epoxy deserves its own section later in this guide — it is genuinely superior in the right application and genuinely unforgiving to apply.

## Matching Grout to Joint Width: The Rules That Prevent Cracking

Joint width is not a style decision; it is a structural one, and the grout type follows from it:

**Under 3 mm — unsanded.** Fine joints need a fine grout; sanded grout cannot pack into a 2 mm gap, and forcing it leaves voids that crack and stain. Narrow joints belong on walls and rectified-edge porcelain where the tile edges are true enough to meet closely.

**3 mm to 12 mm — sanded or polymer-modified sanded.** The sand gives the grout body: it resists the shrinkage that turns a wide unsanded joint into a cracked channel. Floors are almost always in this band; 3 mm for rectified porcelain, 4–5 mm for standard ceramic where size variance between tiles needs the wider joint to hide it.

**Over 12 mm — rethink the joint, not the grout.** A joint that wide means the tiles are mismatched or the layout is absorbing measurement errors into the gaps. Grout that thick cracks on floors regardless of type; the fix is better tile calibration or a deliberate wide-joint design with matching wide-joint grout, not ordinary grout stretched beyond its working width.

**Exterior and wet-area floors** get one extra rule: add 1–2 mm of joint width over the interior equivalent. Wider joints give thermal movement somewhere to go and give the grout more cross-section to flex in. The pool deck tiled with tight interior joints is the pool deck that loses its grout in its first dry season.

## Colour Selection for Nigerian Conditions

Grout colour is a maintenance decision disguised as an aesthetic one. Three considerations, in order of how often they are ignored:

**Light grout shows everything; dark grout shows dust itself.** Pure white grout in a Lagos dust environment is grey within a year no matter how often it is mopped — airborne laterite is relentless. Mid-tone colours — beige, greige, tan, grey — age best: they start close to the colour of the dust that will inevitably live in them.

**Contrast ages worse than harmony.** A dark grout line in a light tile field shows every inconsistency in joint width and every hairline crack at 10× the visibility of a tone-on-tone choice. If you want definition, choose a shade darker than the tile, not a different hue.

**Approve the colour from a cured sample, not from the powder or the wet mix.** Grout dries significantly lighter than it looks wet, and powder colour is not joint colour. Have the tiler grout a small area or a sample board, let it cure a day, and approve from that — ideally under both daylight and the room's actual lighting. Re-grouting a whole floor because the wet colour was approved is the most expensive colour mistake in tiling.

One genuinely useful hybrid: on floors, a mid-grey grout with a quality penetrating sealer keeps the joint colour close to the tile tone while shedding most of the staining that makes people regret grout at all.

## Mixing Grout Correctly: The Part Most People Rush

Cement grout is a chemical product with a specified water ratio on the bag, and the two common shortcuts both produce failed joints:

**The right sequence.** Measure the water per the bag (typically around 2.5–3 litres per 10 kg, but the bag's figure governs), add the powder to the water — not water to powder — and mix with a slow-speed drill paddle to a smooth, toothpaste-like paste with no lumps. Let it slake for 2–3 minutes, then remix briefly. The slake matters: it lets the cement fully wet out and the additives activate.

**The two shortcuts that fail.** First, "improving" a stiffening mix with more water. Grout that has started setting is already curing; retempering it with water dilutes the cement paste and yields weak, dusty joints that erode under mopping within months. When grout stiffens, it is done — discard it. Second, mixing a whole 20 kg bag for a job that will take an hour: grout has a pot life (typically 30–60 minutes), and working dead grout into joints leaves exactly the weak, cracked joints the retempering shortcut leaves.

Mix small, work small, mix again. A 5 kg batch through the whole room beats a 20 kg batch half of which is applied after it should have been binned.

## Application Technique: Packing Joints, Not Smearing Them

Grouting is a packing operation, not a painting one. The goal is joints filled to their full depth with compacted grout, and the technique that achieves it:

1. **Confirm the joints are ready.** Adhesive fully set per its data sheet (24 hours minimum for most), joints raked clean of adhesive squeeze-out and dust, tile faces wiped. Grouting over fresh adhesive traps moisture in the joint and compromises both materials.
2. **Load the float and pack diagonally.** Hold the rubber grout float at 45° to the tile and sweep it diagonally across the joints, pressing hard enough to compact grout fully into each gap. Diagonal sweeping keeps the float edge from digging fresh joints out as you pass.
3. **Work in sections you can clean in time.** Grout an area of roughly 4–6 m², or whatever your grout's open working time covers, then clean it before moving on. The whole-room-then-clean approach leaves a cured grout film that costs a day of acid washing to remove.
4. **Tool the joints before they set.** Once grout has stiffened to touch-pressure (10–20 minutes), strike the joints with a small tool or rounded stick — or simply smooth them with the float's edge — for a uniform, slightly concave profile. Consistent pressure gives consistent width appearance.
5. **Do not over-work wet grout.** Repeatedly wiping wet joints pulls water and fine cement to the surface, leaving a weak, chalky joint face that dusts and stains early.

## Cleaning Off: The Two-Bucket Discipline

Cleaning grout off tile faces is timing plus technique, and the two-bucket method is what separates professionals from one-bucket amateurs:

**First wipe, 15–30 minutes in.** When the grout has lost its wet sheen and resists a light finger touch, wipe with a well-wrung, nearly-damp sponge in a diagonal motion. The sponge should shape the joints, not wash them out — too wet a sponge re-dilutes the joint faces.

**The two buckets.** One bucket of clean water for rinsing the sponge, one for wringing into. When the rinse bucket goes grey, change both. A single bucket redistributes cement slurry across the whole field in a haze film that curing makes semi-permanent.

**Haze removal, next day.** A fine cement haze on the tile face is normal even after good wiping. Remove it the following day with a dry microfibre cloth or, for stubborn haze, a dedicated haze remover — not vinegar or acid on fresh grout, which can etch the joint face before it has fully cured.

## Curing: The Waiting Periods That Make or Break the Joint

Grout gains strength over days, not hours, and the waiting periods have functional reasons behind them:

| Time from grouting | Status | Allowed |
|---|---|---|
| 24 hours | Initial set, still weak | Light foot traffic only, no washing |
| 48–72 hours | Most of early strength | Normal traffic, dry mopping |
| 7 days | Full cure | Wet use: showers, mopping, sealing |

Grouting on Friday and using the shower on Saturday is how joints get the "grout washed out after two weeks" review. In wet areas especially, the 7-day rule before full water exposure is the difference between grout that lasts decades and grout that needs raking out within the first year.

Curing also wants reasonable conditions: shade and light dampness are ideal. Direct sun or harmattan wind dries the surface faster than the joint cures, leaving a weak face on a sound body — cover exterior grout work from direct sun on hot days, and a light damp-mist after a few hours helps in the driest season.

## Epoxy Grout: When It Is Worth the Difficulty

Epoxy grout is chemically inert, fully waterproof, stain-proof, and needs no sealing — it is the correct answer for showers, kitchen splashbacks, counters, and any area where staining is the main threat. It is also unforgiving: pot life of 30–45 minutes, sticky working consistency, and a cleanup that must happen promptly with the right pads, because cured epoxy haze is close to permanent.

The honest trade-off for Nigerian conditions: epoxy costs 2–4× cement grout in materials, and many tilers charge extra for the slower, fussier application. For a full family bathroom, that premium buys joints that never stain, never need sealing, and shrug off mould at the chemical level. For a dry-area living room floor, well-sealed polymer-modified cement grout does the same job for a quarter of the cost, because the threats it faces — dust, traffic — are ones it handles well.

The decision rule: wet or stain-prone areas get epoxy if the budget allows; everything else gets quality cement grout plus conscientious sealing.

## Sealing: The Multiplied Lifespan

Cement grout is porous, and a penetrating sealer is what keeps Nigerian dust, cooking oils, and bathroom moisture out of the pores. Sealing is cheap, fast, and skipped in most installations — usually because it is nobody's line item.

**When:** After full cure — 7 days for cement grout, never before. Epoxy needs no sealer.

**How:** Apply a penetrating (impregnating) sealer over the joints with a small brush or applicator bottle, let it absorb for the manufacturer's dwell time, and wipe excess off the tile faces before it dries. Two thin coats 1–2 hours apart beat one soaking coat.

**Reapplication:** Every 1–3 years for wet areas and kitchen floors, longer for dry rooms. The water-drop test tells you when: a drop of water on the joint should bead; if it absorbs and darkens the joint, reseal.

Sealing is also the correct answer to the most common grout complaint — joints that "used to be lighter." In almost all cases the colour changed because the pores filled; sealed joints keep their approved colour for years. The full cleaning routine that complements sealing is in [our tile maintenance guide](/learn/tile-maintenance-cleaning-sealing-grout-care).

## Repairing Failed Grout, and Reading What Failure Means

Grout failure has two causes with different meanings. Narrow, straight cracks along a single joint line usually mean the joint took movement the grout could not absorb — a wall-floor junction that needed silicone, or a room that needed a movement joint. Raking out and re-grouting with the same rigid material in the same joint recreates the same crack; the correct repair replaces it with flexible sealant or a proper movement joint.

Broad, crumbling, dusty failure across many joints means the grout itself was weak — over-watered, retempered, or applied over un-set adhesive. The repair is the same rake-out, but the prevention is in this guide's mixing section, and the whole batch should be suspect.

Rake joints out with a grout saw or oscillating tool to at least two-thirds depth, vacuum the dust, dampen the old joint faces, and pack new grout per the technique above. A spot repair that follows the full method lasts as long as the original should have.

## Conclusion

Grout rewards the same discipline as every other stage of tiling: the right type for the joint width, a colour approved from a cured sample, mixing to the bag's ratio with no retempering, packing rather than painting, the two-bucket clean, the cure periods respected, and a sealer applied after day seven and reapplied when the water-drop test says so.

None of these steps is expensive; all of them are skip-able — which is precisely how grout earns its reputation as the first thing to fail on a tiled surface. Choose it as deliberately as you chose the tiles, apply it by the sequence above, and the joints outlast the trend that made you choose the tiles in the first place.
